{"authored" [{"question" "q-316588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/assets/cec4f46a70e55735e07cc77fb2013c8d5262ca15feda1ee9eac005a79d38147d.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,(4+2)\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-1)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot 1=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,4\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2a8c1d86158d0bce6141f5941bd280e4068ea6f9a97c0ea498503a64bc1f8988" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316588" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The triangle $D$ has vertices $(2,1),(6,1),(5,4).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/assets/92ee29a22baa10f4af25db8221e7d67c729f5f0f1b5e1b3c37aee7e31a92bdfb.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=4\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-3)=4+3=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,7\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2a8c1d86158d0bce6141f5941bd280e4068ea6f9a97c0ea498503a64bc1f8988" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316612" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "42"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The triangle $D$ has vertices $(1,1),(5,1),(3,4).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/assets/3e2c56766f201095bee0eea101b877e30910c676c998f37d261fd7cf7aabb837.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-2)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,-6\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2a8c1d86158d0bce6141f5941bd280e4068ea6f9a97c0ea498503a64bc1f8988" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316606" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/state.json" "file_sha256" "247103d5788deb0aa52b0d348f5e13c2a47b26635ae4d9517c2b3dfeedebdb99" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/example-7454.json" "file_sha256" "33ce61ff3b3eff90b73d97587fc8ed27370ec5f6d5a6c71547fc1706adcf4d0f" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|\\det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =|8-3| \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/example-7454.json" "file_sha256" "33ce61ff3b3eff90b73d97587fc8ed27370ec5f6d5a6c71547fc1706adcf4d0f" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/assets/e8ff769df4089e6ed9a47d56d7317acf41b8aef6a255abc705c0e7ec31a446ea.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/example-5470.json" "file_sha256" "55ecf32c17cf99589905144705fe9dce1cb4f7ade6f9028449f898c99cdaf701" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5470" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det(T) = 4 \\cdot 2 -  2 \\cdot (-1) = 10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,10\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/example-5470.json" "file_sha256" "55ecf32c17cf99589905144705fe9dce1cb4f7ade6f9028449f898c99cdaf701" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(1,1)$, $(4,5),$ and $(-2,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/example-5471.json" "file_sha256" "d6416d61a42f3975e658121ad2003cdbe839dfbeac627df82ef9a5b765acd2e3" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5471" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/assets/ee8f0cf16983d87fa505303ed6372a3f389518d05b3e13f2e9e2121038792b84.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det(T) = (-7) \\cdot 3 -  5 \\cdot (-2) = -11.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-11|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/example-5471.json" "file_sha256" "d6416d61a42f3975e658121ad2003cdbe839dfbeac627df82ef9a5b765acd2e3" "basis" 1045 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992537/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-12282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/assets/fa0dbfe03050a0ca52f2fbe1988a9e6475cd24437c619dba27a4fed984736fc1.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f176cdba2709a722aa9407779400d5dce2b1d5929a4c932d1d9e78dbfff94ad" "basis" 1183 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-12282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 6=9.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,8\\,|\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f176cdba2709a722aa9407779400d5dce2b1d5929a4c932d1d9e78dbfff94ad" "basis" 1183 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-12282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy 