Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f176cdba2709a722aa9407779400d5dce2b1d5929a4c932d1d9e78dbfff94ad" "basis" 1183 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/assets/6805467a24cb40559f205cbfcecc4f5a34ff2ec6ebda8ae67f997c02f123b53c.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-4)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1=-12-2=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,-14\\,|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f176cdba2709a722aa9407779400d5dce2b1d5929a4c932d1d9e78dbfff94ad" "basis" 1183 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "727b597be255a074562489f7d94444178ebfd48dc8b1705ea2d3779a1a54b6a7" "basis" 1183 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316598" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f176cdba2709a722aa9407779400d5dce2b1d5929a4c932d1d9e78dbfff94ad" "basis" 1183 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316598" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f176cdba2709a722aa9407779400d5dce2b1d5929a4c932d1d9e78dbfff94ad" "basis" 1183 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039564/state.json" "file_sha256" "951272036a9a84b190a992e32fe486e55947e740c8692d517e31cb836257c4b4" "basis" 1373 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039564/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|-10\\cdot 8-9\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =|-80-9| \\\\  & =|-89| \\\\  & =89.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivaria